  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will reign over the region through Saturday. With \nnights starting to get a little longer and some boundary layer \nmoisture in place, areas of valley fog will develop in the \novernight hours, lasting through sunrise. Daily high \ntemperatures expected to be 80s Friday through Sunday. Due to \ngood radiational cooling expected tonight, we have blended the \nNBM10 guidance into the minimum temperature guidance to account \nfor more cooling than an average forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModel ensemble guidance indicates that a broad upper level trough \nwill become established during first half of next week with \nseasonable to slightly below average temperatures and higher chances \nfor precipitation. CAPE and shear values increase and combine from \nSunday afternoon through Tuesday enough to flag that time period for \nthunderstorm activity. The most active day appears to be Tuesday as \nlow pressure drags a frontal system across the area. Convective \nparameters look weaker than recent setups that produced severe \nweather in our region, but the trends bare watching closely. The\nbroad trough will keep a risk of showers going through the end\nof the week.",
